n of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397690 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d694cb78-fd17-5d72-bd5e-fb91875a20a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397690 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=6\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))+x(-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi A &=0 \\\\ 12\\pi B &=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{1}{108\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186397691 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397691 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397691 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397691 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397691 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397691 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397691 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186397695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397691 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9d3c5e72-c342-555b-a87c-e14e208988df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397692 #edb/ref 1080 "n=4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397692 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397692 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26abab5a-e0d3-5a63-b7b9-8c8b067c042b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397693 #edb/ref 1080 "n=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397693 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397693 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1bc27d3d-2228-5745-9f75-6ad665b3203a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397694 #edb/ref 1080 "n=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397694 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397694 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c26489de-65c7-593c-9bc1-121a32b98a82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397695 #edb/ref 1080 "n=3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17